{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And you shall take for yourselves on the first day the fruit of a beautiful tree, branches of palm-trees, and the bough of a thick tree, and willows of the brook.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): these four species grow upon water and require watering more than other fruits, and therefore we were commanded concerning them on the festival of Sukkot, which is the season of the water-libation and is the day of judgment for the rains of the year, so that we may find favor before the Holy One, blessed be He, concerning the water with these four species that point to water. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, received: one etrog, one lulav, three myrtles, and two willows." "<b>And there is</b> to interpret that all this is within the implication of the language of Scripture: one etrog, for thus the language proves — 'fruit' (פרי) is singular, as we say in the Talmud: 'one fruit did the Merciful One say, and not two or three fruits.' One lulav, for thus the word כפות (kafot) is singular in implication — it is written כפת (kafat, defective); and indeed, according to precise language, it should have said כף תמר (a palm-branch); it needed to say כפות תמרים of necessity, for if it had said כף it would imply even a single leaf. Therefore we read כפות, whose meaning is many leaves, and that is the commandment of the lulav. And it is written כפת (defective), because the deficiency is so that you not say that the commandment is two lulavs — the commandment is only one lulav. And the matter will be complete with both the ketiv and the qeri. And the word תמרים (tamarim, 'palms') is plural because they are two, male and female, and if they are not planted thus they will not live, for in all species — whether in plants or in the inanimate — we find male and female by way of nature. Three myrtles — this is 'the bough of a thick tree' (וענף עץ עבות). And from where do we have the implication of Scripture that the essential commandment is three branches of myrtle? For this is not explained in the Talmud at all. Rather, the word עבות ('thick') refers back to ענף ('branch'), and the intention is that there be three branches — that is, three twigs of myrtle. And the meaning of ענף ('branch') is that it be covered with leaves, for if not, it is not a 'branch' but a 'tree,' and the Torah said 'branch of a tree' (ענף עץ). Also, עבות refers as well to the leaves, that they be three; and although the measure of a twig is three handbreadths, that is for the beautification of the commandment, but by Torah law three moist leaves suffice, provided they are at the top of each one. And thus you have learned from the implication of Scripture that the commandment is three myrtle-twigs, and on each, three leaves. And from now on, from where do we know that the leaves must emerge from a single stem? Therefore it is written עבת (avot, defective, without a vav), to say that that myrtle called עבות, whose rule is three branches and three leaves on each, must all emerge from a single stem — this is what they said: 'three, three on one reed' (תלתא תלתא בחד קנא). And two willows — this is 'willows of the brook' (וערבי נחל) — the minimum of willows is two." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> 'The fruit of a beautiful tree' — this is Abraham. 'Branches of palm-trees' — this is Isaac. 'The bough of a thick tree' — this is Jacob. 'And willows of the brook' — this is Joseph. The explanation of this Midrash: it mentions the word פרי ('fruit'), which is singular language, and likens it to Abraham, for thus Abraham began to declare the oneness of YHWH in the world and to make His divinity known, and his words produced fruits, as it is written (Genesis 12:5): ״ואת הנפש אשר עשו בחרן״, and it is written (Ezekiel 33): ״אחד היה אברהם״. 'Tree' (עץ) — just as a tree dries up when water is absent and again produces fruit, so too Abraham after he had despaired begot a son at a hundred years. 'Beautiful' (הדר) — that the Holy One, blessed be He, restored beauty to him in old age; and as they expounded: before Abraham there was no old age, and people could not distinguish between father and son. Abraham was given old age; this is what is written (Genesis 24:1): ״ואברהם זקן בא בימים״, and it is written (Proverbs 20:29): ״והדר זקנים שיבה״. 'Branches of palm-trees' — this is Isaac, on account of his being bound (כפות) upon the altar, for כפות is the language of binding. 'Palms' (תמרים) — from the language of כתימרות עשן ('like pillars of smoke'), on account of his being a perfect elevation-offering, and it is written (Song of Songs 3:6): ״מי זאת עולה מן המדבר כתימרות עשן״. 'The bough of a thick tree' — this is Jacob: just as a branch is covered with leaves, so too Jacob is covered with sons, the twelve tribes. 'And willows of the brook' — this is Joseph: just as this willow is moist, withering, and dry, so too Joseph — in his beginning he was moist (לח), when he was viceroy to the king of Egypt and ruler over the land; withering (כמוש), when his father died in his lifetime and he lacked his saltiness; dry (יבש), when he died before his brothers. And the intention in all this is that we allude, through the commandment of the lulav, to the merit of the patriarchs, and we seek mercy before the Holy One, blessed be He, that He protect us through their merit — for thus we find of Moses, the master of all the prophets, that he prayed through the merit of the patriarchs, as it is said (Exodus 32:13): ״זכור לאברהם ליצחק ולישראל עבדיך״." "<b>And further</b> according to this approach: these four species allude to four limbs in the human body, which are the root of all his actions, whether for all the commandments or for all the transgressions — and they are the eyes and the heart, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'the heart and the eye are two agents of sin,' as it is said (Numbers 15:39): ״ולא תתורו אחרי לבבכם ואחרי עיניכם״. And the lips as well — many commandments, and correspondingly many transgressions, depend upon them. And the spine as well, for it is the root of the body and the power flowing into it from the brain. And this is what they said in the Midrash: the etrog resembles the heart, the lulav the spine, the myrtle the eyes, the willow the lips. And the intention in this is that when a person stumbles in transgressions of these four limbs, he finds atonement through these four species, which correspond to them and resemble them — for in every transgression you will find atonement when a person performs the commandment corresponding to it. And thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded concerning King Herod, who killed thousands and myriads of Israel: he asked Shimon ben Shetah whether it was possible that there be a remedy for him, and he answered him that it is possible if he engages in the building of the Holy Temple, may it be rebuilt speedily in our days. And they said: 'he extinguished the light of the world' — that is, because he killed many of Israel — 'let him go and engage in the light of the world.' And this matter enlightens the eyes of the heart, for there is certainly a remedy for a transgression in the performance of the commandment corresponding to it. And concerning the commandment of the lulav, which resembles the spine, David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 35:10): ״כל עצמותי תאמרנה ה׳ מי כמוך״." "<b>And further</b> in the Midrash: 'The fruit of a beautiful tree' — it has food and taste and fragrance. 'Branches of palm-trees' — it has food but no fragrance. 'The bough of a thick tree' — it has fragrance but no food. 'And willows of the brook' — it has neither food nor taste nor fragrance. The etrog alludes to the righteous, who possess Torah and good deeds. The lulav alludes to those who possess Torah but do not possess good deeds. The myrtle alludes to one who has good deeds but does not have Torah. The willow alludes to one who has neither Torah nor good deeds. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: let them all come as one bundle before Me, and I forgive them all their iniquities. And similar to this we find in the ingredients of the incense, which are possessors of good fragrance, and Scripture commanded to introduce among them galbanum (חלבנה), whose odor is foul — to teach concerning the wicked of Israel that they enter into the category of the righteous, the possessors of good fragrance, and all become one bundle before Him, and He receives them in repentance. And further they expounded concerning these four species that they allude to the four kingdoms, and this is what they said: 'through the merit of the four species in the lulav I save you from the four kingdoms.' The etrog — this is the kingdom of Babylon: just as this etrog resembles gold, so too Babylon (Daniel 2:38): ״רישא די דהבא״ ('the head of gold'). 'Branches of palm-trees' — this is the kingdom of Media: the lulav is long, and Haman was hanged upon a long tree. 'The bough of a thick tree' — this is the kingdom of Greece: just as this myrtle is [...] The etrog dwells in its tree from year to year. 'Branches of palm-trees' alludes to the reward of the World to Come: just as the lulav is tall, so too the reward of the World to Come is great; just as the lulav has its leaves one above the other, so too the rungs of the World to Come are one above the other; and just as the lulav produces its fruit only at the end of seventy years, so too the reward of the World to Come is only at the end of a person's days, of whom it is written (Psalms 90:10): ״ימי שנותינו בהם שבעים שנה״ and so forth. The myrtle has bitterness and its fragrance is good — it alludes to the affliction that is among the ways of repentance, namely that a person should afflict himself and subject his body to fasts and lashes and many restrictions according to the measure of his sin, and that he should suffer in proportion to the measure in which he enjoyed, and with all this, his fragrance ascends to the heavens. And the willow alludes to submission and lowliness, and therefore it does not grow on mountains but grows in the brook, in a lowly and humble place — so that he humble his heart and lower himself, and it be atoned for him. And according to this approach, it is fitting to take the lulav together with the myrtle and the willow in the right hand, to allude to the great reward — the reward of the World to Come prepared for him at the end — together with the affliction and the submission; and the etrog alludes to the reward of this world, to be taken in the left hand, for thus the World to Come is likened to the right and this world is likenened to the left, from what is written (Proverbs 3:16): ״אורך ימים בימינה בשמאלה עושר וכבוד״ — our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: 'to those at His right, length of days; to those at His left, wealth and honor.' And the explanation of 'to those at His right' — for one who walks in commandments toward the right side and intends through them for the reward of the World to Come; 'to those at His left' — for one who walks in them toward the left side and intends through them for the reward of this world: to the former He gives length of days, and to the latter He gives wealth and honor." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): regarding the reason for the four species in the lulav — the moisture of the fruit is its life-force, and therefore we were commanded concerning these plants that they be hadar (beautiful), so that their moisture, which is their life-force, be preserved within them, and together with them we make a sign and token for the ways of life. And therefore Scripture chose all four of these plants, for they are fresh all year round and endure in their moisture more than other fruits. And the Torah given to us at Sinai is called 'the path of life,' and we who received it are called 'alive,' as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:4): ״ואתם הדבקים בה׳ אלהיכם חיים כלכם היום״, and therefore the commandment came that we take these plants, possessors of the vegetative soul in which is the sign of life, to praise with them before the Master of all, who is called 'living Elohim.' And if these plants were dry, there would be in them no sign and token for the ways of life, for the moisture within them is their life-force, like the blood in a living body. And from this they said in the Yerushalmi: a dry lulav is invalid, because it is said (Psalms 115:17): ״לא המתים יהללו יה״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: פרי עץ הדר — the one resembling a heart — alludes to the Glory (Hod) comprised of thirty-two paths, and it is the most beautiful of all beauty, as it is written (Song of Songs 6:10): ״מי זאת הנשקפה כמו שחר יפה כלבנה ברה כחמה״. כפות תמרים is the end of the structure called Yesod, and it is the head of all created beings. And the lulav resembles the spine, for it is the central balancing line. And it said 'תמרים' and not 'תמר,' after what is written (Psalms 92:13): ״צדיק כתמר יפרח״, and it empowers in the measure written of it (Song of Songs 7:8–9): ״זאת קומתך דמתה לתמר״, and this is the sense of 'תמרים'; and therefore it is written 'כפת' on account of its unity, and it mentioned 'תמרים' to allude to both of them. And likewise the etrog, which is a heart. And likewise the lulav, which is the heart of the palm. וענף עץ עבות — Greatness (Gedulah) and Might (Gevurah) and Beauty (Tiferet). And this is what they interpreted in Sefer ha-Bahir: ענף עץ עבות — a parable of a man who has his arms and with them shields his head; behold, his arms are two and his head is three, and that is ענף to the left, עבות to the right, and thus עץ is in the middle. And why is 'עץ' said of it? Because it is the root of the tree. And the hadas resembles eyes, and ערבי נחל — Eternity (Netzach) and Majesty (Hod), which are called 'the teachings of YHWH,' and the willow resembles lips, for thus all the prophets receive prophecy from there, and only to there does their prophecy reach and no further. And the willow is the seventh, and therefore the masters of truth proved: willow, from the language of ערבות, as it is written (Psalms 68:5): ״סולו לרוכב בערבות״, for that is the seventh firmament. In Sefer ha-Bahir: what is ערבי נחל? After the name of the place in which they are fixed, whose name is נחל, meaning that they belong to the נחל of which all seven are called נחל. And they said further there: ערבי נחל corresponds to a person's legs, which are two — end of quotation. And we learn in these four species of the lulav that they return to seven, which correspond to the seven, and it appears that this is the opinion of the Ramban." "<b>And I saw</b> in this matter another opinion from one of the kabbalists, who wrote: פרי עץ הדר is the Glory (Hod) above it, and adjacent to it is כפת תמרים, which is Yesod, above כפת תמרים, and adjacent to it ענף עץ עבות — and they are one upon two: Tiferet and Netzach and Hod. And from here it would have been fitting to validate even two and one — that is, two below and one above — except that the allusion is not intended in it. And according to this, the Righteous One (Tzaddik) stands among the hadasim, and therefore the righteous are called by the name 'hadasim,' because they cleave to the Righteous One who stands among the hadasim, above ענף עץ עבות, and adjacent to it is ערבי נחל — and they are Greatness (Gedulah) and Might (Gevurah)." "<b>And behold,</b> all this comes in an ordered arrangement from below upward; and whether ערבי alludes to Netzach and Hod, as is the opinion of the Rav z\"l, or to Gedulah and Gevurah, as is this other opinion — the willow is the seventh in any case, whether above or below. And all the opinions together — the intention of the lulav with its species is nothing but a likeness to the seventh." "<b>And you must</b> understand that because the lulav (in the Gemara) alludes to 'a righteous man like a palm' (tzaddik ka-tamar), the masters of truth reasoned out the law of the snipping of the lulav and the hadas: the lulav whose tip is snipped is invalid, and with the hadas, even if all three are snipped, it is valid. And it is known that snipping alludes to cutting (kitzutz), and with the hadasim there is no concern about cutting, for it is known that the one is included from the two; also the two opposites receive and draw from above, from Binah, and therefore there is no room for error with the hadasim and no concern about cutting. But with the lulav, which is the Righteous One — Yesod — and it is known of it that the efflux it receives comes from a singular source, and the singular from the two, and the two from Binah — therefore there is room for error with it and concern about cutting, lest a person say of it that it does not need the power that comes from Binah and above, which is the beginning of emanation; therefore they said of it: if its tip is snipped it is invalid, for this would certainly constitute a higher and stronger cutting than the cutting of the hadasim. And how fitting it is to take the lulav, which contains these seven, during the seven days of Sukkot, which correspond to those seven, and through them there is a drawing toward Binah in the matter of the offerings that were brought to draw down. And from this the sages z\"l derived: whoever fulfills the lulav bound in its binding and the hadas in its interweaving, Scripture accounts it for him as though he built an altar and offered a sacrifice upon it, as it is said (Psalms 118:27): ״אסרו חג בעבותים עד קרנות המזבח״, and this is clear. And because the lulav and the etrog allude to 'remember' (zachor) and 'observe' (shamor), therefore the lulav is taken in the right hand and its commandment is by day and not by night, and the etrog in the left, and each is taken in the hand proper to it. And the rule of the waving (na'anu'im) is toward six directions: the four winds of the world and above and below, three times for each direction, which among them all total eighteen wavings — for thus the world is sealed with the seal of the Holy One, blessed be He, which is three letters, and it divides into six — three letters for each direction — which are eighteen letters, and therefore eighteen wavings are required. And even though in the Gemara we find only 'extends and draws back, raises and lowers,' and they derived this from the waving (tenufah), as it is written (Exodus 29:27): ״אשר הונף ואשר הורם״ — extends and draws back to Him to whom the winds belong; raises and lowers to Him to whom the heavens and the earth belong — and through this we arouse toward unification. For because our first father cut apart and sinned with the etrog alone, we do not bring the etrog alone but bring it together with the rest of the fruit, to unify it with all of them and all of them with it, and through this we repair what was distorted and appease the Place (HaMakom) with it." "<b>And reflect</b> on the mystery of the Midrash of R. Tanhuma that I wrote above, and you will see how he called these four species that return seven in the language of the Sages — because they correspond to the seven that were emanated from Wisdom, and with these four species power is drawn down into the attributes. And they raised there the statement of Rava, 'and likewise with the lulav' — one may say that the expression 'bringing out and bringing in' serves toward the four directions, and this is for one whose directions are his own." "<b>And we find</b> that our master Hananel, of blessed memory, wrote: this waving that they said — where were they waving? — 'bringing out and bringing in, lifting up and lowering down'; and thus we have received from the words of our master Hai, and they established the essence of this matter from the Jerusalem Talmud, where we learn there: one must wave three times for each and every thing. Rabbi Zeira raised the question: 'this one — one, and this one — one,' or perhaps 'this one and this one — one' — that is, whether the bringing out and bringing in is one, and it contains four wavings — one bringing in — and there are here nine wavings, for there are in the bringing out and bringing in from east to west and from north to south, and likewise the lifting and lowering three; bringing out and bringing in — one, and lifting — one, and lowering — one, and they are eighteen wavings for the six extremities, three wavings for each and every extremity; and the question was not resolved, and for stringency we do eighteen wavings — thus far the language of the Gaon, of blessed memory. <small>(There is here a garbling of words, and whoever wishes to grasp the content of the matter should examine the Rosh and the codifiers, for I did not wish to lay hand and emend. <i>The printer</i>)</small> And that we bind the lulav with its species is to hint at the unity of the seven; and that we untie it on the seventh day is to hint at the middle line that decides — and we should behold it and intend it: how all beings, all of them, the supernal and the lower, depend upon it, just as the leaves on the lulav are all joined and bound and depend upon the spine; and because of this we must intend toward the seventh on the seventh day, and this is clear. And I have further examined the four species of the lulav, and I have seen that in them there is revealed and hidden, just as in the four letters of the Great Name there is revealed and hidden — for the two are revealed by their names, and they are כפת תמרים and ערבי נחל, and the two — Scripture hid their names, and they are פרי עץ הדר and ענף עץ, until we required the tradition of the Sages, of blessed memory, that they are etrog and hadas. And likewise in the Great Name there is also revealed and hidden: the revealed in יהו and the hidden too in יהו, for the י as written would double in its numerical value in its pronunciation, and likewise the ה and likewise the ו; and from this it emerges for us that the letters of the Great Name indicate that He is one, whether in the revealed or in the hidden. And this matter of revealed and hidden is also alluded to in this commandment, which is a commandment that alludes to the emanation hinted in the unique Name, which is revealed and hidden; and may He, in His mercy, open our eyes to know the wonders of His Torah, and shelter us in the shadow of His mercy and His compassion."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {4 "The text contains a lengthy internal duplication: the passage from 'ודומה לזה מצינו בסימני הקטרת' through 'בזכות ד׳ מינין שבלולב אני מצילכם מד׳ מלכיות' appears twice, and the four-kingdoms section is interrupted by the repetition. Translating the text exactly as received, including the duplication; the four-kingdoms Midrash is left incomplete mid-flow as it appears in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:29:14.049063368Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}